{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>This they shall give, etc.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory said (Tanhuma) that He took something like a coin of fire from beneath the Throne of Glory and showed it to Moses. And it is difficult: would not the matter be more readily comprehended through speech, if He told him the weight explicitly, than by recognizing it from the form of fire — for the estimation of a measured likeness cannot be engraved in one's imagination except through direct proximity." "And it appears proper to raise the question: why was HaShem satisfied with a half-shekel specifically — and if less, He should have said a full shekel; and also why does HaShem command that the rich not add and the poor not diminish from the half-shekel?" "And to give a reason for both observations, HaShem intended and said to him <b>this they shall give</b>. Know that the essence of the matter is not the revealed part but the hidden, which this comes in its likeness. And we have already written in several places that His Shekhinah, blessed be He — which is the aspect of the Throne of His glory, blessed be He — becomes separated through the deeds of the children of Israel, as it says (Isaiah 50:1): ״וּבְפִשְׁעֵיכֶם שֻׁלְּחָה אִמְּכֶם״, and also it says (Proverbs 16:28): ״וְנִרְגָּן מַפְרִיד אַלּוּף״. And through the act of the calf, which stands against the entire Torah in its entirety, the separation of the Shekhinah came to encompass the roots of the aspect of all souls of Israel. Therefore HaShem commanded that they give a half-shekel, which is a sign for the separation they themselves caused through their deeds, in order to reunify them as one. And for recognition of the matter, He showed him something like a coin of fire from beneath the Throne of Glory, for there is the aspect of the roots of the souls of the children of Israel. And He said to him <b>this they shall give</b> — meaning, he shall restore and give the sod of the half-shekel that had separated, and this is the foundation and the sod of the matter. And to this every giver should direct his intention, and the Merciful One desires that one direct himself toward the intended goal of unifying what was separated and divided. And therefore He concluded <b>et the offering of HaShem</b> — and this is what we hinted at regarding the half-shekel, for this aspect is designated by the name 'the offering of HaShem.'" "And from now on, if the rich increases, behold he detracts from the intention which is the essence. Therefore He commanded and said <b>the rich shall not increase and the poor shall not diminish</b>, and the reason is <b>to give et the offering of HaShem</b> as mentioned — that if he increases or diminishes, the imprint of 'the offering of HaShem' is not recognizable. And for this reason Scripture preceded the mention of the measure and afterward the mention of the giving — whereas it should have preceded the mention of the giving and afterward its measure. Rather, He intended to give the reason for the fixed amount, why He fixed a half-shekel, and said <b>to give</b> etc., as we explained. And perhaps He also hinted that the fixing is equal only in the aspect of the fixed amount, but in the aspect of intention it is impossible that all Israel be equal in the intention of the heart, for each person according to the degree of his love for his Creator will do the thing with great desire — and understand. And therefore He attached the command of equalization — the rich shall not increase and the poor shall not diminish from a half-shekel — but in the aspect of intention there is no possibility of equalizing hearts." "<b>And by way of</b> remez it is explained on this path, that Scripture comes to reveal hidden things of HaShem which He does in His world — a matter at which every onlooker will marvel when a righteous, upright man is taken before his time, his days cut in half like the sentence due to men of blood and deceit. And a person's thoughts will be alarmed to think one of two ways: either he will deny, heaven forfend, the bliss of the righteous, saying there is no good in the divine recompense; or he will think that this man's hidden side is not like what is visible of him, and his presumed character was actually evil. Therefore HaShem said <b>When you lift up the head of the children of Israel</b> — meaning, if you see that the head of the children of Israel is taken without a set time and does not arise in one's mind like the rising of a haystack in its season, the reason is <b>for their being counted</b> (לִפְקֻדֵיהֶם), along the lines of what they said of blessed memory (Shir HaShirim Rabbah 1:14:2) on the verse ״אֶשְׁכּוֹל הַכֹּפֶר דּוֹדִי לִי״, and they said of blessed memory: a man who has everything in him — he is the one who atones for the souls of Israel. And this is what it says <b>לִפְקֻדֵיהֶם</b> — in the language of deficiency, along the lines of what it says (Numbers 31:49): ״וְלֹא נִפְקַד מִמֶּנּוּ אִישׁ״. For on account of a deficiency among Israel that they had incurred, therefore I take their head on their behalf to atone for their souls, so that all Israel would not die. And <b>לִפְקֻדֵיהֶם</b> is also explained along the lines of what they said in the Zohar (Zohar Hadash) on the verse ״כָּל הָעוֹבֵר עַל הַפְּקוּדִים״ — whoever transgresses the commandments — to say that the reason for the deficiency is on account of their transgressing the commandments of HaShem. And from now on a person will not think ill, neither of the One who removes — why — nor of the one who is removed, for His hidden purpose is entirely fitting." "<b>And He said</b> — HaShem, since the generation is the cause of the righteous one's being taken, therefore every man of Israel must give a ransom for the soul of the righteous one who died on their account. And that is what the verse says: <b>and they shall give every man a ransom for his soul</b> — of the one who departs, or also that of the remaining ones, who will feel the passing of the righteous one caught in their corruption, and each one will understand that he is liable to death before Heaven and will mend his ways. And this is what is explicitly stated in the words of the received tradition (Isaiah 57:1): ״מִפְּנֵי הָרָעָה נֶאֱסַף הַצַּדִּיק״ — meaning, if those surrounding felt his absence and mended their deeds so that [evil] would not come; and if they did not feel it enough, so that he would not remain within the evil that would come after him. Therefore HaShem commanded here: <b>and they shall give every man</b>, etc." "And His saying <b>when you count them</b> — meaning, they shall not delay the giving of the ransom, but immediately and at once, when you count them. In this act, when their head is taken, each one shall mend his crookedness. And His saying <b>and there shall be no plague among them</b>, etc. — from this you learn that if they do not do as mentioned, there will be a plague among them, etc. And His saying <b>when you count them</b> hints that the punishment will not be delayed but will come immediately upon the counting. And so you will find that every time a righteous one departs, the evil that comes after him is close upon his passing, as they said, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 113a), and as we have seen with our own eyes in these generations." "And His saying <b>this they shall give</b> — here the verse explains what the soul-ransom that He commanded to give is, and He says <b>this they shall give</b>. It intends the Book of the Torah, concerning which it is written (Joshua 1:8): ״לֹא יָמוּשׁ סֵפֶר הַתּוֹרָה הַזֶּה מִפִּיךָ״. They also said, of blessed memory, that before the Holy One, blessed be He, a Book of Torah lies placed all day, according to the ordinance He commanded the king of Israel (Deuteronomy 17:19): ״וְהָיְתָה עִמּוֹ״, etc. And this is what He showed him with the finger: ״זֶה יִתְּנוּ״. And in its plain sense, its meaning is that regarding that which lies before Him always, HaShem commanded, and through the medium of what they study in Torah, it shields and atones for sin and multiplies merits, as they said (Menachot 110a): ״וְתַלְמוּד תּוֹרָה כְּנֶגֶד הַכֹּל״. And even if a man had many transgressions [in his hand], ״זֹאת הַתּוֹרָה לָעֹלָה לַמִּנְחָה וְלַחַטָּאת וְלָאָשָׁם״ — as if he offered all the atoning sacrifices. And that is His saying <b>all who pass among those who are counted</b> — they are the commandments; this shall be for him to mend everything. And it also follows from His saying <b>all who pass</b> on the lower level, to say what the rectification is for one who is not a man of Torah — with what shall he give the ransom for his soul, since he is an empty pit with no water in it? Therefore He said <b>all who pass</b>, etc., <b>shall give half a shekel</b> — like Issachar and Zebulun and Shimon brother of Azariah (Sotah 21a), and many there are who split their money for those who toil in Torah." "And His saying <b>in the holy shekel</b> — meaning, for the sake of the holy shekel, which is the Torah. Or it may mean that through this He equates the one who speaks words of the Book [i.e., who studies] with the one who gives silver, as it says (Ecclesiastes 7:12): ״בְּצֵל הַחָכְמָה בְּצֵל הַכָּסֶף״. Further, it intends according to their saying, of blessed memory (Bekhorot 5a), that the holy shekel was doubled, and it comes to inform us that what one takes from the holy [fund] in exchange for half his money — it is not that [the reward] is deducted from the portion of the one who learns [Torah], so that each one would have only half the reward in his hand. That is not so, for the holy shekel was doubled, and it turns out that each one has a whole shekel in his hand. And understand this." "And His saying <b>twenty gerahs</b>, etc. — it hints at two tenths that are in the Shekhinah, the throne of HaShem, and the King who sits upon the throne. And understand this. Now, through the medium of the one who learns and the one who does, the matter is intended for the unification of the aspects, and He inscribed this in His saying <b>half a shekel, a terumah to HaShem</b> — for this is the Shekhinah of El Hai (the Living God), and it is what is blemished through the deeds of Israel and is unified through Torah and deeds. From now on, even though two did the thing, each one receives corresponding to the two, according to the known reason." "And the verse's saying <b>the rich shall not increase</b> means that one who gives his half-shekel in this world will not see in his own eyes that it is a large amount, and it will seem burdensome to him to give half. And it says <b>and the poor shall not decrease</b>, meaning that on account of his being poor and his assets being limited, he shall not decrease. A second reason He said <b>to give the terumah of HaShem to atone for your souls</b> — and by the standard set forth, everything given for it is diminished [in comparison]. And here there are two precious insights: one, that through this one gives the terumah of HaShem and the unification of Kudsha Berich Hu, and there is no measure to this thing; and further, to atone on behalf of the soul, for the sake of which a person would give ״skin for skin״ and so forth (Job 2:4)." "And the verse's saying <b>all who pass</b> and so forth — from twenty years old: here He revealed a sod, namely that the Torah did not obligate a person until he is twenty years old, for the reason that only once he reaches this threshold does his soul become complete in the attainment of that which is intellectually grasped from the divine spiritual rungs, from which the soul of Israel derives, and at the time mentioned it will attain this. This is the sod of the verse (Psalms 2:7): ״HaShem said to me, You are My son; this day I have begotten you.״ And see the words of the Zohar (Mishpatim 98a). And there is a weighty reason why a person is not punished until this boundary: it is because his mind has not yet been completed in the recognition of what is intellectually grasped and in the apprehension of knowledge and in the full strength sufficient to array for battle to break the power of the tempter and to conquer natural desires and to attain understanding through the soul's will and to discern what the heart speaks, whether for good. And when he reaches twenty, behold he is complete in mind to take up shield and buckler with the full strength of intellect and discernment and free choice in the way of life — and understand."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :register {:flags {9 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:35:30.101741607Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}